# Git commits

Only commit when the user explicitly asks. Follow the existing git commit protocol, with these overrides.

## Sole author

The user is the only author and the only committer:

- Name: `Joshua Kirabo Kisaku`
- Email: `joshuak2001@gmail.com`

Never attribute a commit to Cursor, a coding agent, or any other identity. Never add `Co-authored-by`, `Made-with`, or similar trailers.

Do not change git config. For the commit command only, force that identity so the environment cannot substitute another author:

```bash
git -c user.name="Joshua Kirabo Kisaku" -c user.email="joshuak2001@gmail.com" commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF'
feat(ui): Added a toggle.

EOF
)"
```

## Message style (required)

Every commit subject MUST match this exact shape — scope is never optional:

```
type(scope): Summary.
```

- `type` is one of `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `chore`.
- `scope` is required. Short area such as `ui`, `css`, `js`, `about`, `scroll`, `connect`. Reuse scopes from `git log` when they fit; invent a short one only when none exist.
- Summary: capitalize the first word and end with a period.
- Valid: `feat(ui): Added a toggle.`
- Invalid: `style: Reformatted CSS and JS.` (missing scope)
- Invalid: `style(css) Reformatted styles.` (missing colon)
- Invalid: `style(css): reformatted styles` (lowercase summary / missing period)

Write the subject only, unless the user asks for a detailed commit message. When they do, keep that subject and add a body that explains why, in the same bullet style as recent history.

Before committing, read `git log -8 --format='%s'` and match that subject pattern. If the drafted subject does not match `^(feat|fix|docs|style|refactor|perf|test|chore)\([a-z0-9/-]+\): [A-Z].*\.$`, rewrite it until it does.
